    {
      "id": "EV1",
      "node_kind": "Event",
      "type": "Event",
      "label": "Drought Intensification in Europe",
      "is_main": true,
      "evidence": [
        "Human-caused climate change has intensified a drought across Europe, with extreme heat, rather than low rainfall, driving the exceptionally dry conditions, a team of international scientists said on Thursday.",
        "A new study found that today's hotter climate has accelerated moisture loss from Europe's soils, lakes and rivers, increasing the likelihood of bone-dry conditions.",
        "They found that extremely dry soils were five times more likely today in western Europe than in a world without climate change, and 11 times more likely in eastern Europe."
      ],
      "confidence": 0.97,
      "type_oie": "EnvironmentalEvent",
      "type_source": "schema.org",
      "type_vocab_id": null,
      "type_is_new_to_vocab": false,
      "type_definition": null,
      "type_parent": null,
      "type_justification": "Drought intensification is a natural environmental event occurring at a specific time and location"
    },
    {
      "id": "CL1",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Zachariah on Drought Drivers",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "\"Overall, our results show that this drought is not primarily a story of low rainfalls but of a warmer atmosphere that is causing more moisture deficits on the land,\" Zachariah, from Imperial College London, told reporters."
      ],
      "confidence": 0.92
    },
    {
      "id": "CL2",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Schumacher on Heatwave Impact",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "If you have increasingly hot air, it will soak the soils dry. And then it gets even hotter, and the soils get even drier,\" he said. This rapid drying also primed the land for wildfires, he added."
      ],
      "confidence": 0.91
    },
    {
      "id": "CL3",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Study on Climate Change Effects",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "The planet has warmed about 1.4C since 1850–1900, primarily because of the burning of coal, oil and gas.",
        "Scientists say climate change is making destructive events such as droughts, floods and heatwaves more frequent and intense, and that Europe is the world's fastest-warming continent."
      ],
      "confidence": 0.93
    }
